---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Robotic Pool Cleaners",
    "url": "/best-robotic-pool-cleaner/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Pool Heaters",
    "url": "/best-pool-heater/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Hot Tubs",
    "url": "/best-hot-tub/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Pool Deck Sealers",
    "url": "/best-pool-deck-sealer/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Pressure Washers",
    "url": "/best-pressure-washer/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Garden Hoses",
    "url": "/best-garden-hose/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-10-19", modified: "2026-10-19" });
const title = "7 Best Pool Vacuums: Suction, Pressure-Side and Cordless Handheld Picks";
const description =
  "The best pool vacuums for inground and above-ground pools, from suction-side and pressure-side cleaners to cordless handhelds, compared on hose, pump needs and fit.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "hayward-poolvac-xl",
    "name": "Hayward PoolVac XL",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "category": "Suction-Side, Inground, Up to 20 x 40 ft",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The PoolVac XL is the cleaner we would start with for a typical inground pool, because it does the work with equipment you already own. Hayward's spec sheet describes it as running off the existing filtration system, so there is no booster pump to buy, plumb or pay to run, and it connects to the skimmer or a dedicated suction port. Hayward rates it for pools up to 20 by 40 feet and ships it with a 40-foot hose, and the sheet lists coverage of the floor, walls and coves using its AquaPilot steering and a pod-and-shoe traction system. Hayward sells separate versions for gunite and for vinyl pools, which matters because the shoes differ, so buy the one for your surface. Hayward lists a two-year warranty, while one Australian listing for the gunite model shows one year, so check the terms where you buy. The limits are the ones every suction cleaner shares: it pulls debris into your skimmer basket and pump strainer rather than its own bag, so a pool that drops heavy leaf loads will fill those baskets quickly.",
    "pros": [
      "Runs off the existing pump, so no booster pump is needed, per Hayward",
      "Rated for pools up to 20 x 40 ft with a 40-ft hose, per the spec sheet",
      "Floor, walls and coves listed as covered",
      "Separate gunite and vinyl versions so the shoes suit your surface",
      "Connects to a skimmer or dedicated suction port without tools, per Hayward"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Sends debris to the skimmer basket and pump strainer, which need emptying more often",
      "Not suited to heavy leaf loads",
      "Warranty listed as 2 years by Hayward but 1 year on one regional listing"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For a typical inground pool without a leaf problem, the Hayward PoolVac XL is the easiest win: it runs off the pump you have, Hayward rates it for pools up to 20 by 40 feet, and it ships with a 40-foot hose."
  },
  {
    "id": "zodiac-baracuda-g3",
    "name": "Zodiac Baracuda G3",
    "badge": "Best for Variable-Speed Pumps",
    "category": "Suction-Side, Inground, All Surfaces",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The Baracuda G3 earns its place for owners who have moved to a variable-speed pump and want to run it slowly. Leslie's describes it as compatible with low-speed pump settings and credits its FlowKeeper valve with regulating flow automatically, which is the part that keeps a suction cleaner moving when the pump is turned down to save energy. It is a suction-side cleaner for inground pools that Leslie's lists for all pool surfaces, and the kit includes 39 feet of hose, the FlowKeeper valve, a hose weight, a flow gauge and an elbow connector, so most installs need nothing else. Like other suction cleaners it uses your pump and your skimmer basket rather than a bag of its own. Leslie's notes it is best on small and medium debris, and its own customer comments mention larger leaves as a weakness that a skimmer net covers. We did not find a published maximum pool size, so judge reach by the 39-foot hose and confirm the rating in Zodiac's manual for your pool.",
    "pros": [
      "FlowKeeper valve regulates flow, which helps at low variable-speed settings, per Leslie's",
      "Listed for all inground pool surfaces",
      "Kit includes 39 ft of hose, flow gauge, hose weight and connector",
      "No booster pump required"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No published maximum pool size in the listings we found",
      "Best on small and medium debris; large leaves are a listed weak spot",
      "Fills the skimmer basket like any suction cleaner"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "If you run a variable-speed pump at low speed, the Zodiac Baracuda G3 is the suction cleaner built for it, with a FlowKeeper valve to steady the flow and 39 feet of hose in the box."
  },
  {
    "id": "pentair-kreepy-krauly-great-white",
    "name": "Pentair Kreepy Krauly Great White GW9500",
    "badge": "Best for Sloped Pools",
    "category": "Suction-Side, Inground, 15-in Path",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The Great White is the suction cleaner we would look at for a pool with a deep end that drops steeply. One retailer describes it as best suited to dish, incline and hopper-bottom pools, and Pool360 lists a 15-inch cleaning path, an adjustable regulator valve with a flow gauge for setting its speed, and a free vac-port door fitting for installation into a dedicated vacuum line. It is listed for gunite, fiberglass, vinyl and specialty surfaces, and Pool360 shows a two-year warranty and a unit weight of 21.5 pounds. One area where sources disagree is the hose: Pool360 lists 40 feet while another retailer describes 32 feet of hose sections, so measure from your skimmer to the far corner and check the box before you rely on either figure. Pricing has also been inconsistent, with one retailer listing it near $650 and another showing a lower sale price while out of stock, so compare current offers.",
    "pros": [
      "Wide 15-in cleaning path, per Pool360",
      "Adjustable regulator valve with flow gauge for setting speed",
      "Listed as suited to dish, incline and hopper-bottom pools",
      "Two-year warranty listed by Pool360",
      "Works on gunite, fiberglass, vinyl and specialty surfaces, per retailers"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Hose length conflicts between listings (40 ft versus 32 ft)",
      "Heavier than most suction cleaners at 21.5 lb listed",
      "Stock and pricing have been patchy"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For a pool with a steep hopper or dish-shaped deep end, the Pentair Great White's 15-inch path and adjustable regulator make it the suction cleaner to shortlist, but confirm the hose length for your pool."
  },
  {
    "id": "polaris-vac-sweep-280",
    "name": "Polaris Vac-Sweep 280",
    "badge": "Best Pressure-Side",
    "category": "Pressure-Side, Inground, Booster Pump Required",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The Vac-Sweep 280 is the classic answer to a leafy pool. As a pressure-side cleaner it collects debris in its own bag instead of sending it to your pump, which is exactly what you want when leaves would otherwise clog the skimmer basket. Polaris's product page says it works in all inground pools, connects to a dedicated pressure line, and requires a booster pump, and its spec table lists the booster pump requirement for each version. That requirement is the whole decision: if your pool already has a booster pump and a dedicated cleaner line, the 280 drops in; if it does not, budget for the pump, the plumbing and the electricity to run it. Polaris's troubleshooting guidance says the cleaner should produce two distinct, even jets of water when running, which is a quick way to confirm the pump is delivering enough pressure, and it advises keeping the metal pre-filter in place and clear. We did not find the required pump pressure in the listings, so check the owner's manual for the exact figure.",
    "pros": [
      "Collects debris in its own bag, sparing the pump basket",
      "Works in all inground pools, per Polaris",
      "Simple check: two even jets mean the pump pressure is right, per Polaris",
      "Long-running design with wide parts availability"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Requires a booster pump and a dedicated pressure line, per Polaris",
      "Booster pump adds purchase, plumbing and running cost",
      "Required pump pressure not stated on the product page"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "If your pool already has a booster pump, the Polaris Vac-Sweep 280 is the leaf-friendly choice because it bags debris itself instead of loading your skimmer."
  },
  {
    "id": "polaris-vac-sweep-3900-sport",
    "name": "Polaris Vac-Sweep 3900 Sport",
    "badge": "Best Premium Pressure-Side",
    "category": "Pressure-Side, Inground, 2.5-in Inlet",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The 3900 Sport is the pressure-side cleaner for the pool that gets hit hardest by leaves and seed pods. Polaris lists three venturi jets, a 2.5-inch vacuum inlet, all-wheel PosiDrive with a stainless-steel chain, automatic back-up and a dual-chamber SuperBag, plus a TailSweep PRO that scrubs as it moves. The wide inlet is the feature that matters: bigger debris goes into the bag instead of jamming the throat. Polaris says a compatible Polaris booster pump is required, and an older retailer listing names the PB4-60 as an example and says the pump adds about 40 gallons per minute of filtration through the bag. The warranty is the weak spot in our research: the only term we found was one year, from a retailer, rather than Polaris's own documentation, so confirm it before buying. Like the 280, it only makes sense if you already have, or are willing to add, a booster pump.",
    "pros": [
      "2.5-in vacuum inlet and three venturi jets, per Polaris",
      "Dual-chamber SuperBag holds heavy leaf loads",
      "All-wheel PosiDrive with stainless-steel chain",
      "Automatic back-up helps it work out of corners"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Compatible Polaris booster pump required",
      "Warranty terms found only on a retailer listing",
      "Costs considerably more than a basic suction cleaner"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For a heavily treed pool with a booster pump already in place, the Polaris 3900 Sport's 2.5-inch inlet and dual-chamber bag handle the leaf load that chokes smaller cleaners."
  },
  {
    "id": "water-tech-pool-blaster-max-li",
    "name": "Water Tech Pool Blaster Max Li",
    "badge": "Best Cordless Handheld",
    "category": "Cordless Handheld, Pools and Spas, 10.5-in Head",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The Pool Blaster Max Li is for jobs an automatic cleaner never does well: steps, benches, spas, small above-ground pools and the pile of debris that collects in one corner. Water Tech lists up to 60 minutes of runtime on its rechargeable lithium battery, a 10.5-inch head and a unit weight of 5.05 pounds, and it attaches to a standard telescopic pole so you vacuum from the deck. Leslie's lists a four-hour recharge and a price of $199.99, and Water Tech lists a one-year limited warranty. Because it is cordless and self-contained, it needs no hose, no pump connection and no booster, which is why it suits spas and pools with no suction port. Water Tech also sells a commercial-grade CG version with a 13-inch head and a stronger pump, which Leslie's says gives 33 percent more power and suction, if you want to clean a larger pool by hand. The trade-off is labour: you are still the one walking it around.",
    "pros": [
      "Up to 60 minutes per charge, per Water Tech",
      "No hose, pump connection or booster needed",
      "Light at 5.05 lb, per Water Tech",
      "Fits a standard telescopic pole",
      "Commercial-grade CG version available with a 13-in head"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Manual: you do the walking",
      "Four-hour recharge, per Leslie's",
      "One-year limited warranty"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For steps, spas and small pools, the Water Tech Pool Blaster Max Li is the simplest tool there is: up to an hour of cordless suction with no plumbing at all."
  },
  {
    "id": "aiper-pilot-x1",
    "name": "Aiper Pilot X1",
    "badge": "Best Value Handheld",
    "category": "Cordless Handheld, Dual Filters, 1,260 GPH",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The Pilot X1 is a cordless handheld for owners who want spot cleaning at a lower cost than the commercial-grade handhelds. Retail listings describe 1,260 gallons per hour of suction, a dual filtration system with 360-micron and 100-micron stages, and an anti-clog system, and they list a working depth from about 7.9 inches to 8 feet, which covers steps, shallow ends and most deep ends. Like the Pool Blaster, it needs no hose or pump, which makes it a sensible companion to an automatic cleaner that misses the steps. We did not find a published runtime for the X1 in the listings we checked, so look for that figure on the box or Aiper's page before you buy, and compare it with the roughly 45 minutes Aiper lists for its PilotFlow V3 if you want a reference point. Treat it as a spot cleaner rather than the only cleaner for a large pool.",
    "pros": [
      "1,260 GPH suction listed by retailers",
      "Dual 360-micron and 100-micron filtration",
      "Works from about 7.9 in to 8 ft deep, per listings",
      "No hose, pump or booster needed"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Runtime not published in the listings we found",
      "Manual operation, like any handheld",
      "Better as a spot cleaner than a whole-pool solution"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The Aiper Pilot X1 is the budget-friendly cordless handheld for steps and spot cleaning, with 1,260 GPH suction and fine dual filtration listed, but check its runtime before buying."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Pool Vacuums",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-pool-vacuum/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best pool vacuum for an inground pool?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most inground pools without heavy leaf fall, a suction-side cleaner such as the Hayward PoolVac XL is the best value because it runs off your existing pump. Hayward rates it for pools up to 20 by 40 feet with a 40-foot hose. Leafy pools are better served by a pressure-side cleaner with its own bag."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need a booster pump for a pool vacuum?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only for most pressure-side cleaners. Polaris says the Vac-Sweep 280 and 3900 Sport require a booster pump. Suction-side cleaners run off your filter pump, and cordless handhelds and robots need no pump at all, so most pools can avoid a booster entirely."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a suction or pressure pool cleaner better?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Suction cleaners are cheaper and best for fine debris, but they load your skimmer and filter. Pressure cleaners bag leaves and larger debris themselves, but usually need a booster pump. Choose by your debris and by whether a booster pump is already installed."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use a pool vacuum in an above-ground pool?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, if the model is rated for it. Many suction cleaners come in above-ground versions, and cordless handhelds such as the Water Tech Pool Blaster Max Li suit small above-ground pools and spas because they need no plumbing. Check the manufacturer's rating for your pool type."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long should a pool vacuum run each day?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most owners run an automatic cleaner for two to three hours a day during the swimming season, timed with the filter pump. Pools with heavy debris may need longer. Check your skimmer and pump baskets after each run, because a full basket stops a suction cleaner moving."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are cordless pool vacuums worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For spot cleaning, steps, spas and small pools, yes. Water Tech lists up to 60 minutes of runtime for the Pool Blaster Max Li, and handhelds need no hose or pump. For a large pool, they work best as a companion to an automatic cleaner because you do the walking."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long do pool vacuums last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "With regular part replacement, suction and pressure cleaners often run for several seasons. Diaphragms, shoes, tyres and bags wear out and need replacing. Warranties in the listings we found run one to two years, so buy from a brand with good parts availability."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Pool & Spa",
    tags: [
      "best pool vacuum",
      "pool vacuum",
      "automatic pool cleaner",
      "suction pool cleaner",
      "pressure side pool cleaner"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Pool Vacuums</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Suction-side, pressure-side and cordless handheld cleaners, chosen by the pump you already own, the pool you have and the debris that actually lands in it.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#hayward-poolvac-xl"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Shaheer Ali Khan</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Consumer Product Testing &amp; Materials Performance Specialist</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="8.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#three-types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Suction, Pressure or Handheld</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Picks</a>
          <a href="#pump" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Your Pump Decides</a>
          <a href="#debris" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Match the Cleaner to the Debris</a>
          <a href="#surface" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Pool Shape and Surface</a>
          <a href="#setup" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Setup and Hose Care</a>
          <a href="#cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Running Cost and Upkeep</a>
          <a href="#robotic" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. When to Skip to a Robot</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best pool vacuum for most inground pools is a suction-side cleaner that runs off the pump you already have, such as the Hayward PoolVac XL, because it costs a fraction of a robot and needs nothing extra installed. If your pool sits under trees and collects leaves, a pressure-side cleaner with its own debris bag is the better tool, but it usually needs a booster pump. And if you only need to spot-clean steps, a spa or an above-ground pool, a cordless handheld does the job without any plumbing at all.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run these cleaners in a test pool. We compared what manufacturers and the largest pool retailers publish: the type of cleaner, whether it needs a booster pump, the rated pool size, hose length, cleaning path, warranty and any special features that change how it behaves in a real pool. Where two sources gave different numbers for the same model, we say so in the product card rather than choosing the more flattering figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            We deliberately kept this list to cleaners that work with your pool's own plumbing, plus two cordless handhelds. Robotic cleaners are a separate category with a separate budget and their own buying rules, so they have their own guide: if you are ready to spend more for a cleaner that runs independently of your pump, read <a href="/best-robotic-pool-cleaner/">our robotic pool cleaner picks</a> instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every pick here also had to be easy to buy and easy to service. Suction and pressure-side cleaners wear out shoes, tyres, diaphragms and bags, so we favoured models from Hayward, Zodiac, Pentair and Polaris, whose replacement parts are stocked by almost every pool store. A cheap cleaner with no parts support becomes landfill after its first worn diaphragm.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, we sorted the picks by the decision that matters most, which is your pump. The single biggest mistake people make with pool vacuums is buying a pressure-side cleaner for a pool with no booster pump, or a suction cleaner for a pool that drops a bushel of leaves every week. The sections below walk through those choices in order.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="three-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Suction, Pressure or Handheld
          </h2>

          <p>
            A suction-side cleaner plugs into your skimmer or a dedicated vacuum port and is pulled around the pool by the suction your filter pump already makes. It has no motor of its own and no debris bag; everything it picks up goes to the skimmer basket, the pump strainer and then the filter. That makes it the cheapest automatic option to buy and to run, and the right choice for pools with fine debris such as dust, sand and small leaves.
          </p>

          <p>
            A pressure-side cleaner connects to a return line and is pushed around by water pressure, usually from a dedicated booster pump. It carries its own debris bag, so leaves and twigs never reach your pump or filter. That is why pressure-side cleaners dominate in pools under trees. The trade-off is the booster pump: if your pool does not already have one, you are adding a second pump, a dedicated line and the electricity to run it.
          </p>

          <p>
            A handheld cordless vacuum is a self-contained battery unit on the end of a pole. It needs no plumbing and no pump, which makes it perfect for spas, steps, benches, small above-ground pools and the corner where debris collects. It is also the only option for a pool with no suction port at all. The trade-off is that you do the walking, so it is a companion to an automatic cleaner rather than a replacement for one in a large pool.
          </p>

