        <div id="pump" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Your Pump Decides
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before choosing a cleaner, look at your equipment pad. If you see a small second pump next to the main filter pump, usually plumbed to a dedicated line and often on a timer, you already have a booster pump, and a pressure-side cleaner such as the Polaris 280 or 3900 Sport will drop straight in. If there is only one pump, a suction-side cleaner or a handheld is the sensible choice unless you are prepared to add a booster.
          </p>

          <p>
            Adding a booster pump is not a small job. It usually needs a dedicated return line to the pool wall, a new circuit or timer connection, and a pump that the cleaner maker approves. That is plumbing and electrical work, and it adds a second motor to your electricity bill for every hour the cleaner runs. For many pools, the honest answer is that the money is better spent on a robotic cleaner, which needs no booster at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Variable-speed filter pumps change the suction-side picture too. Running a variable-speed pump at low speed saves a lot of electricity, but it also reduces the suction that drags a suction cleaner around. Cleaners with a flow-regulating valve, such as the Zodiac Baracuda G3 with its FlowKeeper, are designed to cope with that. If your cleaner stalls when the pump slows down, raise the speed for the cleaning window rather than running it flat out all day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you buy, set the cleaner's flow with the gauge or regulator that comes in the box. Too much suction makes a suction cleaner climb out of the water or stick to the floor; too little leaves it sitting still. Polaris's simple check for its pressure cleaners, two distinct, even jets of water, is a good reminder that the right pressure is something you measure rather than guess.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="debris" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Match the Cleaner to the Debris
          </h2>

          <p>
            The debris that lands in your pool should drive the choice as much as the pump. Fine debris, such as dust, pollen and sand, is what suction cleaners handle best, because the filter catches particles far smaller than any bag. A pool in an open yard with little tree cover is ideal suction-cleaner territory.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leaves change everything. A suction cleaner that swallows a leaf load sends it all to the skimmer and pump baskets, which clog, reduce flow and leave the cleaner stalled. A pressure-side cleaner with a large bag and a wide inlet, such as the Polaris 3900 Sport with its 2.5-inch inlet and dual-chamber bag, keeps leaves out of the plumbing entirely. If leaves are your main problem, prioritise that inlet size and bag capacity.
          </p>

          <p>
            Seasonal debris matters too. A pool that is clean most of the year but takes a heavy hit in autumn can often be handled with a suction cleaner plus a leaf net or a handheld for the worst weeks. Buying a booster pump and pressure cleaner purely for one bad month is rarely good value.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not forget what lands outside the water. Leaves and grit on the deck end up in the pool, so keeping the surround clean reduces the load. A sealed, cleanable deck helps; our guide to <a href="/best-pool-deck-sealer/">pool deck sealers</a> covers the coatings that make that easier, and a <a href="/best-pressure-washer/">pressure washer</a> makes quick work of the deck before the season starts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="surface" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Pool Shape and Surface
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pool surface matters because cleaners use different shoes, tyres and brushes for different finishes. Hayward, for example, sells separate versions of the PoolVac XL for gunite and for vinyl pools. Using a gunite cleaner on a vinyl liner can wear or mark the liner, and a vinyl cleaner on rough plaster wears out faster. Buy the version listed for your surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pool shape matters too. Pools with a steep hopper or dish-shaped deep end are hard for some cleaners to climb out of. Retailers describe the Pentair Great White as suited to dish, incline and hopper-bottom pools, which is why we include it. Free-form pools with lots of curves and steps reward cleaners with programmed steering, such as Hayward's AquaPilot system.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pool size sets the hose length you need. The rule of thumb is that the hose should reach from the skimmer or suction port to the farthest point of the pool with a little slack. Hayward rates the PoolVac XL for pools up to 20 by 40 feet with a 40-foot hose. Measure your pool before buying, and remember that extra hose sections can be added, while a hose that is too short leaves the far end dirty.
          </p>

          <p>
            Above-ground pools are a special case. Many suction cleaners are sold in inground and above-ground versions, and some owners report using inground cleaners on above-ground pool floors, but manufacturer ratings are the safe guide. For small above-ground pools, a cordless handheld is often the simplest answer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Setup and Hose Care
          </h2>

          <p>
            Setting up a suction cleaner takes minutes. Fill the hose with water first by holding each section against a return jet until no bubbles come out; air in the hose can make the pump lose prime. Then connect the hose to the skimmer adapter or vac port, set the flow with the regulator or gauge, and let the cleaner sink. Most makers, including Hayward, describe installation as tool-free.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pressure-side cleaners connect to the booster pump's dedicated wall fitting. Check the in-line filter or pre-filter before each use, because a clogged screen starves the cleaner of pressure. Polaris specifically advises keeping the metal pre-filter in place and clear.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hose care decides how long a cleaner keeps working well. Hoses develop memory and coil, which makes cleaners steer in circles. Store hoses straight when the cleaner is out of the pool, and replace kinked or cracked sections. A <a href="/best-garden-hose/">good garden hose</a> is also worth having to rinse the cleaner and bag after each use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Take the cleaner out when you swim, shock the pool or vacuum to waste. Leaving it in during high chlorine levels shortens the life of hoses, diaphragms and shoes, and it gets in the way of swimmers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Running Cost and Upkeep
          </h2>

          <p>
            Suction cleaners have almost no running cost beyond the pump you already run. They do increase how often you empty the skimmer and pump baskets and backwash or clean the filter, so factor that time in. Wear parts are the real cost: diaphragms, shoes, tyres and skirts wear out with use and need replacing every season or two.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pressure-side cleaners add the cost of the booster pump's electricity every hour they run, plus bags, tyres and tail scrubbers. If you already have a booster pump, that cost is part of your routine. If you are adding one, include the pump, plumbing and electrical work in the comparison against a robotic cleaner.
          </p>

          <p>
            Handheld cordless vacuums cost only the electricity to charge them and replacement filters. Their main cost is your time, and batteries eventually lose capacity like any lithium battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            Warranties are short across this category, typically one to two years in the listings we found. Keep the receipt, register the cleaner, and buy from a dealer who stocks parts. For cleaning the deck and equipment pad, a <a href="/best-shop-vac/">shop vac</a> is handy for sucking water out of pump baskets and equipment pits.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="robotic" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When to Skip to a Robot
          </h2>

          <p>
            A robotic cleaner makes sense when you want the pool cleaned without loading your pump, filter or skimmer at all. Robots have their own motor, filter and power supply, so they clean the floor, walls and often the waterline while your pump runs at whatever speed suits your energy bill.
          </p>

          <p>
            Robots also make sense when the alternative is buying a booster pump. Once you add the cost of the pump, the plumbing and the electrician to a pressure-side cleaner, a mid-range robot is often similar money with better cleaning and no extra pump to run.
          </p>

          <p>
            The downside is upfront cost and fragility. Robots cost several times more than suction cleaners, need their filters cleaned after every cycle, and are expensive to repair outside warranty. Our <a href="/best-robotic-pool-cleaner/">robotic pool cleaner guide</a> compares corded and cordless models in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever cleaner you choose, it is only one part of keeping the water comfortable. If you swim outside the warmest months, our guide to <a href="/best-pool-heater/">pool heaters</a> explains the running-cost trade-offs, and for a smaller, warmer soak, see <a href="/best-hot-tub/">our hot tub picks</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Pressure Cleaner Without a Booster Pump</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most pressure-side cleaners, including the Polaris 280 and 3900 Sport, require a booster pump. Buying one for a pool with only a filter pump means adding a pump, a dedicated line and wiring, which can cost more than the cleaner itself. Check your equipment pad first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Suction Cleaner in a Leaf-Heavy Pool</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Suction cleaners send everything to the skimmer and pump baskets. In a pool under trees, those baskets clog quickly, flow drops, and the cleaner stops moving. A pressure-side cleaner with its own bag, or a robot, is the better tool for leaves.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Flow Setting</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Too much suction makes a cleaner climb out or stick; too little leaves it still. Use the regulator, flow gauge or valve that comes in the box, and recheck it after you clean the filter or change pump speed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the Hose Coil</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Coiled hoses steer cleaners in circles and leave parts of the pool untouched. Store hoses straight, add or remove sections to match the pool, and replace kinked sections rather than living with them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Wrong Surface Version</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gunite and vinyl versions use different shoes and tyres. The wrong version can mark a vinyl liner or wear out fast on plaster. Check the model number against your pool surface before ordering.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

