        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best pool vacuum for an inground pool?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most inground pools without heavy leaf fall, a suction-side cleaner such as the Hayward PoolVac XL is the best value because it runs off your existing pump. Hayward rates it for pools up to 20 by 40 feet with a 40-foot hose. Leafy pools are better served by a pressure-side cleaner with its own bag.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a booster pump for a pool vacuum?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only for most pressure-side cleaners. Polaris says the Vac-Sweep 280 and 3900 Sport require a booster pump. Suction-side cleaners run off your filter pump, and cordless handhelds and robots need no pump at all, so most pools can avoid a booster entirely.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a suction or pressure pool cleaner better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Suction cleaners are cheaper and best for fine debris, but they load your skimmer and filter. Pressure cleaners bag leaves and larger debris themselves, but usually need a booster pump. Choose by your debris and by whether a booster pump is already installed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a pool vacuum in an above-ground pool?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if the model is rated for it. Many suction cleaners come in above-ground versions, and cordless handhelds such as the Water Tech Pool Blaster Max Li suit small above-ground pools and spas because they need no plumbing. Check the manufacturer's rating for your pool type.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should a pool vacuum run each day?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most owners run an automatic cleaner for two to three hours a day during the swimming season, timed with the filter pump. Pools with heavy debris may need longer. Check your skimmer and pump baskets after each run, because a full basket stops a suction cleaner moving.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cordless pool vacuums worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For spot cleaning, steps, spas and small pools, yes. Water Tech lists up to 60 minutes of runtime for the Pool Blaster Max Li, and handhelds need no hose or pump. For a large pool, they work best as a companion to an automatic cleaner because you do the walking.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do pool vacuums last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With regular part replacement, suction and pressure cleaners often run for several seasons. Diaphragms, shoes, tyres and bags wear out and need replacing. Warranties in the listings we found run one to two years, so buy from a brand with good parts availability.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

